<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herod Antipas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 20 BCE–after 39 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Herod Antipas, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a>, ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peraea">Peraea</a> as tetrarch from 4 BCE to 39 CE. He built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> as his capital, had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a> executed and appears in the Gospel accounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>'s trial. In 39 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a> deposed and exiled him, giving his lands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-agrippa-i">Herod Agrippa I</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herod-antipas.jpg" alt="Herod Antipas" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georges Reverdy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herod_Antipas_medal.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Antipas was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a> and Malthace, a woman from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>. His date of birth is unknown, but he, his full brother Archelaus and his half-brother Philip were educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. He was not Herod's first choice of heir. That place went to Aristobulus and Alexander, sons of the Hasmonean princess Mariamne, until they were executed, and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a>, who was convicted of trying to poison his father. In Herod's final will Archelaus was to be king, while Antipas would rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peraea">Perea</a> as tetrarch. The three heirs travelled to Rome, where Augustus largely confirmed the division of territory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peraea">Perea</a> were separated by the Decapolis, and both were unsettled. While Antipas was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, dissidents had attacked the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sepphoris">Sepphoris</a>, and Quinctilius Varus, the Roman governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, burned the town and sold its inhabitants as slaves. Antipas rebuilt and fortified Sepphoris and added a wall to Betharamphtha in Perea, later called Livias and then Julias. His main construction was his capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> on the western shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-galilee">Sea of Galilee</a>, named for the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>. Pious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> at first refused to live there because it was built over a graveyard, so Antipas settled it with foreigners, forced migrants, poor people and freed slaves. His coins carried no images, out of respect for Jewish prescriptions against idolatry.</p><p>He divorced his first wife Phasa'el, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aretas-iv">Aretas IV</a> of Nabatea, in order to marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodias">Herodias</a>, who had been married to his half-brother Herod II. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a> condemned the marriage, and Antipas had him imprisoned at Machaerus and beheaded. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> says instead that John's influence made Antipas fear rebellion. In Luke's account Pilate, learning that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> was a Galilean, sent him to Antipas, who questioned and mocked him and sent him back. Some scholars doubt that this trial took place.</p><p>The divorce added to disputes with Aretas over the border, and in a war around 36 Antipas's army was defeated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> ordered a counter-offensive under Lucius Vitellius, but it was abandoned when Tiberius died in March 37.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Antipas's nephew Agrippa, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodias">Herodias</a>, became a friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a> and received Philip's former tetrarchy with the title of king in 37. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, Herodias urged Antipas to ask for the title of king too, but Agrippa accused him before Caligula of conspiring with Sejanus and with King Artabanus. In the summer of 39 Antipas was exiled and his territory passed to Agrippa. Josephus names Lugdunum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and, elsewhere, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> as the place. He died in exile at an unknown date.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Herod Antipas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Herod's death in 4 BCE, Augustus divided the kingdom among his sons Archelaus, <b>Herod Antipas</b> and Philip.</blockquote><a href="/state/herodian-kingdom-of-judea">Herodian Kingdom of Judea</a></li><li><blockquote>After the death of Herod the Great in 4 BCE, Augustus gave his son <b>Herod Antipas</b> two separate territories: Galilee, west of the Sea of Galilee, and Peraea, a strip east of the Jordan.</blockquote><a href="/state/tetrarchy-of-galilee-and-peraea">Tetrarchy of Galilee and Peraea</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judea</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugdunum-convenarum">Lugdunum Convenarum</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.7,35.31&amp;year=18" aria-label="Judea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Herod Antipas"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5386 4846h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5386 4846h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.7,35.31&amp;year=18">What was here in 18 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
